k my list is good. Let me also reconsider if any facts should be combined or if I'm missing anything.\n\nMissing considerations:\n- The sequence of events: message → shoulders sag → sit down → loosen teeth → breathing longer. These are sequential but each is already captured as a separate fact.\n- The causal relationship: the message caused 沈北川's physical reactions. But the text doesn't explicitly state causation, it just describes the sequence. I shouldn't infer causation.\n\nI think my list of 12 facts is comprehensive. Let me finalize.\n\nWait, actually, I want to reconsider fact 5. \"差点掉进泥里\" - this is an event that almost happened but didn't. The status should be... hmm. The near-miss itself happened (it's a fact that the board almost fell). So \"沈北川的登记板差点掉进泥里\" is 已发生 (the near-miss occurred). But if I phrase it as \"沈北川的登记板掉进泥里\" that would be 否定 (it didn't fall). I think phrasing it as \"差点\" with status 已发生 is correct.\n\nActually, I realize I might be overthinking this. Let me just check: is the near-miss really a useful fact for future retrieval? It tells us 沈北川 had a physical reaction (losing grip on the board) due to emotional response to the message. But the more important facts are that he was holding a board and that he reacted to the message. I'll keep it but it's lower priority.\n\nOK, final answer. Let me also make sure I don't have speaker fields on pure narrative facts. Only the walkie-talkie message needs a speaker.\n\nOne more thing: for fact 11, \"泥水渗进裤脚\" - the text says \"泥水渗进裤脚\" without specifying whose pants. But since 沈北川 is the only character mentioned and he just sat on the ground, it's clearly his pants. I'll phrase it as \"泥水渗进沈北川裤脚\".\n\nAlright, I'm confident in my final list. Let me output the JSON.",
